# Full TPTP ATP Benchmark on Helma

This notebook runs a large-scale ATP benchmark on the Helma HPC cluster using
the TPTP problem library.

The following provers are compared:

- LEO-II
- Zipperposition
- E-Prover
- Vampire
- LEO-III
- ShotTx

The benchmark uses the full TPTP problem set and executes the workload in
smaller chunks so that the generated Slurm arrays remain within the cluster
limits.

---

## 1. Install dependencies

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:atp_benchmark_runner,
   github: "penthooose/atp_benchmark_runner",
   force: true}
])
```

---

## 2. Configure the environment

The setup form collects the HPC username, SSH key and cluster configuration.

```elixir
setup =
  AtpBenchmarkRunner.prepare_livebook_setup(
    submit_label: "Setup"
  )

env_map = setup.env_map
env_file = setup.env_file
```

---

## 3. Connect to Helma

```elixir
boot =
  HpcConnect.bootstrap(
    mode: :local,
    env_file: env_file,
    cluster: env_map["HPC_CONNECT_CLUSTER"] || "helma",
    username: env_map["HPC_CONNECT_USERNAME"],
    key_path: env_map["HPC_CONNECT_IDENTITY_FILE"]
  )

session =
  %{boot.session |
    steady_connection: false,
    retry_forever: true
  }

IO.inspect(
  %{
    cluster: session.cluster.name,
    ssh_alias: session.ssh_alias,
    username: session.username,
    work_dir: session.work_dir,
    vault_dir: session.vault_dir
  },
  label: "Resolved HPC session"
)
```

The benchmark uses ordinary SSH connections with automatic retries. This is
useful when the cluster gateway temporarily refuses or closes connections.

---

## 4. Load the full TPTP problem set

The benchmark is pinned to TPTP 9.3.1 for reproducibility.

```elixir
tptp_archive = %AtpBenchmarkRunner.TPTP.Archive{
  id: :tptp_full,
  label: "TPTP v9.3.1 full library",
  version: "9.3.1",
  url: "https://tptp.org/TPTP/Distribution/TPTP-v9.3.1.tgz",
  archive_name: "TPTP-v9.3.1.tgz",
  default?: true,
  components: [
    :problems,
    :axioms,
    :generators,
    :documents,
    :utilities
  ]
}

archive_result =
  AtpBenchmarkRunner.ensure_tptp_archive(
    archive: tptp_archive
  )

IO.inspect(
  archive_result,
  label: "TPTP archive"
)
```

### 4.1 Load problems and select provers

```elixir
problems =
  AtpBenchmarkRunner.load_tptp_problems(
    include_axioms: false
  )

selected_provers = [
  :leo2,
  :zipperposition,
  :eprover,
  :vampire,
  :leo3,
  :shot_tx
]

available_provers =
  AtpBenchmarkRunner.Provers.names()

missing_provers =
  selected_provers -- available_provers

if missing_provers != [] do
  raise "Unavailable provers: #{inspect(missing_provers)}"
end

summary =
  AtpBenchmarkRunner.TPTP.summarize(problems)

IO.inspect(
  summary,
  label: "TPTP problem summary"
)

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("Problems: #{length(problems)}")
IO.puts("Provers: #{length(selected_provers)}")

IO.puts(
  "Prover/problem combinations: " <>
    "#{length(problems) * length(selected_provers)}"
)

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("Selected provers:")

Enum.each(
  selected_provers,
  fn prover ->
    IO.puts("  - #{prover}")
  end
)
```

### 4.2 Check Slurm array limits

```elixir
array_config =
  HpcConnect.connect!(
    session,
    """
    scontrol show config | grep -E 'MaxArraySize|MaxJobCount'
    """
  )

IO.puts(array_config)
```

---

## 5. Prepare and validate prover images

### 5.1 Resolve prover definitions

```elixir
prover_structs =
  Enum.map(
    selected_provers,
    &AtpBenchmarkRunner.Prover.builtin!/1
  )

IO.puts("Resolved #{length(prover_structs)} prover definitions.")
```

### 5.2 Prepare the standard prover images

ShotTx is handled separately in the next step because its source download
requires explicit proxy handling on Helma.

```elixir
standard_provers =
  Enum.reject(
    prover_structs,
    &(&1.name == :shot_tx)
  )

standard_images =
  AtpBenchmarkRunner.build_prover_images!(
    session,
    standard_provers,
    force: false,
    build_on_login_node: false,
    build_cluster: nil,
    partition: "preempt_cpu",
    walltime: "04:00:00",
    cpus: 8,
    timeout: 14_400_000
  )

IO.inspect(
  standard_images,
  label: "Standard prover images"
)
```

Existing images are reused because `force: false`.

### 5.3 Prepare the ShotTx image

ShotTx downloads its source during the Apptainer build. On Helma, the Erlang
HTTP client needs the cluster proxy configured explicitly.

This creates a temporary definition file. It does not modify the prover
definition stored in the repository.

```elixir
priv_dir =
  :atp_benchmark_runner
  |> :code.priv_dir()
  |> to_string()

original_shot_def =
  Path.join([
    priv_dir,
    "provers",
    "shot_tx",
    "apptainer.def"
  ])

patched_shot_def =
  Path.join(
    System.tmp_dir!(),
    "shot_tx_helma.def"
  )

original =
  File.read!(original_shot_def)

patched =
  if String.contains?(original, ":https_proxy") do
    original
  else
    String.replace(
      original,
      "      :inets.start()\n",
      """
            :inets.start()
            :httpc.set_options([
              {:proxy, {{~c"proxy.nhr.fau.de", 80}, []}},
              {:https_proxy, {{~c"proxy.nhr.fau.de", 80}, []}}
            ])
      """,
      global: false
    )
  end

patched =
  String.replace(
    patched,
    "{:timeout, 60000}",
    "{:timeout, 300000}",
    global: false
  )

File.write!(
  patched_shot_def,
  patched
)

unless String.contains?(patched, ":https_proxy") do
  raise "ShotTx proxy configuration could not be prepared."
end

IO.puts(
  "Temporary ShotTx definition: #{patched_shot_def}"
)
```

Build or reuse the ShotTx image:

```elixir
shot_sif =
  HpcConnect.build_sif_blocking(
    session,
    "shot_tx",
    local_def_path: patched_shot_def,
    force_rebuild: false,
    build_cluster: nil,
    build_on_login_node: false,
    use_slurm: true,
    partition: "preempt_cpu",
    walltime: "02:00:00",
    cpus: 8,
    timeout: 7_200_000
  )

IO.puts(
  "ShotTx image: #{shot_sif}"
)
```

### 5.4 Smoke-test all six provers

Before starting the full benchmark, each prover is executed on a small
compatible TPTP example.

```elixir
smoke_results =
  AtpBenchmarkRunner.smoke_validate_images!(
    session,
    prover_structs,
    timeout_seconds: 30
  )

Enum.each(
  smoke_results,
  fn result ->
    IO.inspect(
      %{
        prover: result.prover,
        problem: result.problem_id,
        ok?: result.ok?,
        exit_status: result.exit_status,
        szs_status: result.szs_status
      }
    )
  end
)
```

Every prover should report `ok?: true` before continuing.

---

## 6. Prepare the full benchmark

The full problem set is divided into chunks of 5,000 problems.

This keeps each Slurm array below Helma's configured maximum array size.

```elixir
chunk_size = 5_000

benchmark_chunks =
  Enum.chunk_every(
    problems,
    chunk_size
  )

IO.puts(
  "Total problems: #{length(problems)}"
)

IO.puts(
  "Number of chunks: #{length(benchmark_chunks)}"
)

IO.puts("")

benchmark_chunks
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.each(
  fn {chunk, index} ->
    combinations =
      length(chunk) *
        length(selected_provers)

    IO.puts(
      "Chunk #{index}: " <>
        "#{length(chunk)} problems " <>
        "(#{combinations} prover/problem runs)"
    )
  end
)
```

The benchmark configuration uses:

- one CPU per prover/problem task;
- 4 GB memory per task;
- the `preempt_cpu` partition;
- a 30-second prover timeout;
- bounded parallel Slurm arrays;
- sequential chunk submission.

```elixir
benchmark_options = [
  mode: :hpc,
  hpc_mode: :multi_node,
  partition: "preempt_cpu",
  max_parallel_jobs: 16,
  cpus_per_task: 1,
  ntasks: 1,
  mem: "4000M",
  exclusive: false,
  timeout_seconds: 30,
  walltime: "00:10:00",
  wait_for_completion: true,
  poll_interval_ms: 60_000,
  max_wait_ms: 86_400_000,
  prepare_images: false,
  extended_debug: false
]

IO.inspect(
  benchmark_options,
  label: "Benchmark configuration"
)
```

---

## 7. Run the full benchmark

The launch switch is deliberately disabled by default.

Change it to `true` only when the setup, images, smoke tests and chunk plan have
been verified.

```elixir
launch_full_benchmark? = false
```

```elixir
chunk_runs =
  if launch_full_benchmark? do
    benchmark_chunks
    |> Enum.with_index(1)
    |> Enum.map(
      fn {chunk, index} ->
        IO.puts("")
        IO.puts(
          "========================================"
        )

        IO.puts(
          "Starting chunk #{index}/#{length(benchmark_chunks)}"
        )

        IO.puts(
          "Problems: #{length(chunk)}"
        )

        IO.puts(
          "Prover/problem runs: " <>
            "#{length(chunk) * length(selected_provers)}"
        )

        IO.puts(
          "========================================"
        )

        options =
          Keyword.put(
            benchmark_options,
            :title,
            "Full TPTP benchmark - chunk #{index}"
          )

        plan =
          AtpBenchmarkRunner.bootstrap(
            session,
            selected_provers,
            chunk,
            options
          )

        IO.puts(
          "Run ID: #{plan.id}"
        )

        results =
          AtpBenchmarkRunner.run_benchmark(
            plan
          )

        IO.puts(
          "Chunk #{index} collected " <>
            "#{length(results)} results."
        )

        %{
          chunk: index,
          run_id: plan.id,
          plan: plan,
          results: results
        }
      end
    )
  else
    IO.puts(
      "Benchmark launch is disabled."
    )

    IO.puts(
      "Set launch_full_benchmark? = true " <>
        "after validating the previous sections."
    )

    []
  end
```

---

## 8. Combined benchmark report

Combine the results from all completed chunks.

```elixir
all_results =
  Enum.flat_map(
    chunk_runs,
    & &1.results
  )

IO.puts(
  "Collected results: #{length(all_results)}"
)
```

Generate the aggregated comparison:

```elixir
full_report =
  if all_results == [] do
    IO.puts(
      "No benchmark results are available yet."
    )

    nil
  else
    report =
      AtpBenchmarkRunner.report(
        all_results
      )

    AtpBenchmarkRunner.print_report_markdown(
      report
    )

    report
  end
```

### 8.1 Per-prover summary

```elixir
if full_report do
  IO.puts(
    "| Prover | Total | Solved | Failed | Solve Rate |"
  )

  IO.puts(
    "|--------|------:|-------:|-------:|-----------:|"
  )

  Enum.each(
    full_report.by_prover,
    fn result ->
      IO.puts(
        "| #{result.prover} | " <>
          "#{result.total} | " <>
          "#{result.solved} | " <>
          "#{result.failed} | " <>
          "#{Float.round(result.solve_rate * 100, 1)}% |"
      )
    end
  )
end
```

The full TPTP library contains several logical languages. Not every prover is
intended to support every logic. Unsupported-input statuses should therefore be
distinguished from genuine attempts when interpreting the final comparison.

---

## 9. Recover results from an interrupted session

Every submitted HPC run has a run ID.

If the Livebook session is interrupted after a chunk has been submitted, its
results can be collected without submitting the benchmark again.

### Most recent submitted run

```elixir
# recovered_results =
#   AtpBenchmarkRunner.collect_last_hpc_results!(
#     session
#   )
#
# IO.puts(
#   "Recovered #{length(recovered_results)} results."
# )
```

### Specific run ID

```elixir
# recovered_results =
#   AtpBenchmarkRunner.collect_hpc_results!(
#     session,
#     "run_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS_ID"
#   )
```

---

## 10. Optional cleanup

The temporary SSH key uploaded through the Livebook setup can be removed after
the work is complete.

A persistent SSH key in the user's normal `.ssh` directory is not deleted.

```elixir
# AtpBenchmarkRunner.cleanup_livebook_setup()
```
